`context/learning/<concept-id>.md` is the **lesson** for one wiki concept: the
composition that turns a page into something teachable and assessable. It is a
document, not a runtime — `sdt learn lesson <concept>` composes it and
`sdt learn record` is the only writer of what a learner demonstrated.

Read it with `instructions/wiki.md` (the page it teaches) and
`instructions/learning.md` (how a session runs). This module covers the artefact
only.

## What a lesson is

- **One concept, one lesson, the same id.** The lesson id equals the wiki page id
  (`context/learning/architecture/four-key-metrics.md` teaches
  `wiki/architecture/four-key-metrics`), and its `concept` field repeats it so a
  lesson read alone names its page.
- **The page owns the knowledge.** Objectives, difficulty and pacing live in the
  page's `learning:` block, and this is its whole shape:

  ```yaml
  learning:
    objectives:
      - "name the four metrics and the pipeline point each is measured at"
      - "calculate a metric over its reporting window"
    difficulty: intermediate   # introductory | intermediate | advanced
    minutes: 12                # 5-60, the review time
  ```

  A lesson never restates them: an item points at one by its 1-based index, so
  `objective: 2` means the second entry above and nothing else.
- **The lesson's own frontmatter is written by `context new --type lesson`**:
  `kind: lesson`, `uid`, `id` (equal to the concept id), `concept` (the same id,
  repeated so the file names its page), `summary`, `status`, the timestamps,
  `project`, and `links`/`sources` pointing at `wiki/<concept-id>.md`. The
  lesson's `status` is its own lifecycle, not a teaching verdict: `sdt learn
  check` judges the page.
- **A lesson is not a page.** No claim is introduced here. Every item names the
  claim it rests on (`<page-id>#claim-N`) and every example names the span it was
  re-expressed from.

## Sections, in this order and no others

`## Prerequisites` · `## Worked example` · `## Assess` · `## Transfer`

- **Prerequisites** — the curricular order, written as what to learn first and
  why. This is not `relations.depends_on`, which is logical dependency; a lesson
  states what a learner needs before this one.
- **Worked example** — one example, carried through to its answer, with its
  provenance in `source_span` (`refs/<file>@<sha>:<start>-<end>`: the sha pins the
  converted refs file, and the numbers are **line** offsets in it). Re-express the
  example in our words and cite the span; do not paste the source. This is the one
  place where numbers, thresholds and arithmetic the page deliberately omits are
  allowed, because they are the lesson's subject. **Cite and re-express is the
  rule**: one single clause may be quoted verbatim where the exact wording carries
  the meaning (a definition, a threshold), marked as a quote with the same span;
  everything longer is re-expressed. The decision is the user's, 2026-10-28 —
  `context/questions/20261028-070641-lesson-example-licensing.md`.
- **Assess** — the item table, one row per item.
- **Transfer** — one case the learner has not seen, posed without its solution.

## The item table

```markdown
| id | objective | type | prompt | expected | rubric | distractor | claim |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| four-key-metrics-1 | 1 | recall | Name the four metrics. | deployment frequency; lead time for changes; change failure rate; time to restore service | all four, no extras | the four are individual performance targets | architecture/four-key-metrics#claim-1 |
```

- **`objective`** is 1-based against the page's `learning.objectives`; every
  objective needs at least one item, and an index outside the list is a defect.
- **`claim`** is `<page-id>#claim-N`, where `<page-id>` is the id **without** the
  `wiki/` prefix (`architecture/four-key-metrics#claim-1`) — the same id the
  lesson's own frontmatter carries.
- **`type`** is `recall`, `diagnose`, `apply` or `transfer`, and it *is* the
  item's difficulty ladder in that order. It is not restated as a number. An
  `apply` item may rest on the worked example — that is what the example is for —
  but its rubric says so, and a page-only evaluation excludes it.
- **`expected`** is the answer, not a hint: precise for recall, with its tolerance
  for a numeric apply (`2/36 = 5.56% (±0.1 percentage points)`). State the unit and
  the rounding once; a rate over one day and the reported metric over its window
  are two different numbers, so an item says which one it asks for. An answer the
  page does not determine is marked **not in the corpus**, never filled from
  recall. **Write the expected answer from the page, never from the span**: the
  worked example may carry what the page leaves out, an item's key may not.
- **`distractor`** is a real wrong model — the page's counter-case or a
  boundary — never a random option. A distractor nobody holds teaches nothing.
- **`claim`** is the page and claim the expected answer rests on. An item whose
  claim does not support its expected answer is the defect this column exists to
  surface.
- At least one item per lesson is `transfer`: the same idea in a case the page did
  not cover. The `## Transfer` **section** poses open work for a tutor to run; the
  `transfer` **item** is the assessable form of it. A lesson has one section and
  one such item — not two obligations.

## Check before you ship

```bash
sdt learn lesson <concept>              # the composed bundle
sdt learn lesson --validate             # every lesson: claim anchors, objective coverage, shape
sdt learn lesson --coverage             # objective → item matrix
sdt learn check <concept>               # is this concept teachable, and if not why
```

`sdt learn check` refuses a page that cannot carry a lesson — not `active`, fewer
than four claims, `maturity: wip`, no `learning:` block, no lesson, an objective
without an item. It judges the **page**; a lesson's own `status` is its lifecycle
and is not part of the verdict. A **missing `verified_at` is not a refusal**: a
stamp is provenance, not validity, and it is reported as a note. `--verified-only`
filters on it when a caller wants a freshness subset.

## Boundaries

- One lesson per concept; a second lesson for the same page is a split signal, not
  a variant.
- No answers stored in the page, no objectives stored here, no learner state in
  either: `context/learning/learner.yaml` holds what a learner demonstrated.
- Never promote a page to `active` because a lesson needs it. The page is verified
  against its spans first (`wiki.md`), and the lesson follows.
